    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Classification of Railway Services (Rules 106 & 107, IREC Vol. I)\n- **Group 'A' (Gazetted):** Recruited through Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) via Civil Services Examination, Engineering Services Examination (ESE), Combined Medical Services (CMS).\n- **Group 'B' (Gazetted):** Promoted from eligible Group 'C' employees through 70% Selection (Centralized CBT) and 30% Limited Departmental Competitive Examination (LDCE CBT).\n- **Group 'C' (Non-Gazetted):** Covers Pay Levels 1 to 7 (Grade Pay 1800 to 4600).\n- *Note:* Erstwhile Group 'D' (GP 1800 / Level 1) was completely merged into Group 'C' following 6th and 7th CPC recommendations.\n\n### 2. Recruitment Machinery: RRB vs RRC\n| Attribute | Railway Recruitment Board (RRB) | Railway Recruitment Cell (RRC) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Jurisdiction** | 21 Boards covering territorial zones | 16 Cells, one in each Zonal Railway HQ |\n| **Recruitment Levels** | Level 2 to Level 7 (NTPC, JE, SSE, ALP, Technicians) | Level 1 (Erstwhile Group 'D' - Trackman, Pointsman, Helpers) |\n| **Head of Agency** | Chairman (SAG officer appointed by Railway Board) | Chairman / RRC (Dy. CPO level officer) |\n| **Quotas Managed** | General direct recruitment quotas | Level 1, Act Apprentices (20%), Sports, Scouts & Cultural |\n\n### 3. Compassionate Ground Appointments (CGA - Master Circular 16)\n- **Eligibility:** Dependent family member (widow, son, unmarried daughter, adopted son/daughter) of a railway servant who:\n  1. Dies while in service (in harness).\n  2. Is medically decategorized / invalidated under **Rule 1302 of IREC Vol. II**.\n  3. Is missing for more than 2 years (after police FIR and non-traceable report).\n- **Priority Categorization:**\n  - **Priority I:** Deceased due to railway accident while performing official duties.\n  - **Priority II:** Deceased in service due to medical illness, snake bite, or sudden demise.\n  - **Priority III:** Medically invalidated with left-over service.\n- **Time Limits:** Applications normally accepted within **5 years** from date of death. DRM has powers to condone delay up to 2 years beyond 5 years; General Manager has powers up to **20 years** with Railway Board approval.\n\n### 4. Central Training Institutes (CTIs) of Indian Railways\n- **NAIR (National Academy of Indian Railways, Vadodara - Est. 1930):** Apex training academy for probationary Group A officers, Group B induction, and executive leadership.\n- **IRICEN (Indian Railways Institute of Civil Engineering, Pune - Est. 1959):** Track technology, long welded rails (LWR), prestressed concrete bridges, tunnel engineering.\n- **IRISET (Indian Railways Institute of Signal Engg & Telecom, Secunderabad - Est. 1957):** Electronic Interlocking (EI), Kavach automatic train protection, optical fiber communications, IP networks.\n- **IRIMEE (Indian Railways Institute of Mech & Elec Engg, Jamalpur - Est. 1888):** Rolling stock, diesel/steam traction, workshop technology, Special Class Railway Apprentices (SCRA).\n- **IREET (Indian Railways Institute of Electrical Engg, Nashik - Est. 1988):** 25 kV AC traction, electric locomotives (WAP-7, WAG-9, WAG-12), overhead equipment (OHE).\n- **IRITM (Indian Railways Institute of Transport Management, Lucknow - Est. 2003):** Traffic operations, commercial management, logistics, freight corridors.\n\n### 5. Training Under the Apprentices Act, 1961\n- Governed by **Section 22(1)** of the Apprentices Act, 1961.\n- Indian Railways engages thousands of trade apprentices annually across Carriage & Wagon shops, Loco shops, and Signal workshops.\n- **20% Horizontal Reservation:** Under Ministry of Railways policy, 20% of vacancies in direct recruitment to Level 1 posts are reserved for Course Completed Act Apprentices (CCAAs).\n- **1/3rd Weightage:** CCAAs are granted 1/3rd weightage of marks scored in National Council of Vocational Training (NCVT) examinations while preparing the final CBT merit list.\n\n### 6. APAR & SPARROW Architecture\n- **Statutory Disclosure:** Following the Supreme Court's landmark ruling in *Dev Dutt v. Union of India (2008)*, every entry and numerical grading in the APAR must be communicated to the employee.\n- **Grading Scale:** 1 to 10 (1.0 to 3.9: Below Average, 4.0 to 5.9: Good, 6.0 to 7.9: Very Good, 8.0 to 10.0: Outstanding).\n- **SPARROW:** Electronic system with digital time-stamping, ensuring that reporting, reviewing, and accepting formalities are completed strictly within DoPT prescribed calendars.\n- Representation against adverse or below-benchmark remarks must be submitted within **15 days** of disclosure.\n",
