    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Seniority, Lien & Transfer Rules\n\n#### A. Seniority Principles (IREM Vol. I Chapter III)\n- **Direct Recruits (Para 303):** Seniority is determined by the order of merit confirmed by RRB/training institute examination at the end of the initial apprenticeship/probationary training.\n- **Promotees (Para 306):** Seniority is determined by relative position in the select panel. Candidates securing **80% or more marks** are classified as 'Outstanding' and placed on top of the panel in order of original seniority.\n\n#### B. Lien on Post (Rules 240 & 241, IREC Vol. I)\n- **Lien:** The title of a railway servant to hold substantively, either immediately or on the termination of a period of absence, a permanent post to which he has been appointed substantively.\n- **Retention of Lien:** Retained while performing duties of that post, on foreign service/deputation (up to 5 years), on leave, and during joining time.\n- **Termination of Lien:** Can only be terminated when the railway servant acquires a permanent lien on another post substantively, or upon resignation/removal.\n\n#### C. Transfers (IREM Vol. I Paras 226 to 231)\n- **Own Request Transfer:** Permitted after lock-in period. The employee joins at the bottom of the seniority list of that grade in the new railway unit.\n- **Mutual Transfer:** Two railway servants in the same grade exchange units. Under **Para 310**, the senior employee takes the seniority of the junior employee in the new unit (bottom seniority between the two).\n\n### 2. Railway Servants Liberalised Leave Rules, 1949 (Chapter 5, IREC Vol. I)\n| Leave Type | Rule in IREC Vol. I | Entitlement Quantum | Max Accumulation | Key Statutory Conditions |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Leave on Average Pay (LAP)** | Rule 523 | 30 days/year (15 on 1st Jan & 1st Jul) | **300 days** | Encashment up to 300 days at retirement; up to 10 days during service with pass (max 60 days in career) |\n| **Leave on Half Average Pay (LHAP)** | Rule 526 | 20 days/year (10 on 1st Jan & 1st Jul) | **No limit** | Can be commuted on medical certificate (2 LHAP debited for 1 Commuted Leave on full pay) |\n| **Casual Leave (CL)** | Rule 507 | 8 days (office) / 10 days (line staff) | Zero (lapses Dec 31) | Not treated as absence from duty; cannot be combined with LAP or LHAP |\n| **Maternity Leave** | Rule 551 | 180 days per pregnancy | N/A | Admissible to female staff with < 2 surviving children; full leave salary |\n| **Paternity Leave** | Rule 551A | 15 days | N/A | Admissible to male staff with < 2 children within 6 months of delivery |\n| **Child Care Leave (CCL)** | Rule 551E | **730 days** in entire service | N/A | For women & single male parents; 100% pay for first 365 days, 80% for next 365 days |\n| **Study Leave** | Appendix V | Up to 24 months (36 months for doctors) | N/A | Min 5 years service; bond to serve 3 years post return |\n\n### 3. Railway Services (Pass) Rules, 1986 & HRMS e-Pass\n- **Privilege Pass & PTO (Privilege Ticket Order):**\n  - Non-Gazetted staff: 1 set of Pass + 4 sets of PTO per year during first 5 years; 3 sets of Pass + 4 sets of PTO after 5 years.\n  - Gazetted staff (Group A & B): 6 sets of Passes + 4 sets of PTO per year from date of appointment.\n  - PTO allows purchase of ticket at 1/3rd of the total fare.\n- **Post-Retirement Complimentary Pass (PRCP):**\n  - Granted based on qualifying service (minimum 20 years). Group A & B: 3 sets/year; Group C: 2 sets/year.\n- **HRMS e-Pass:** Generates Unique Pass Number (UPN). Integrated directly with CRIS PRS/IRCTC for online berth reservation.\n\n### 4. Hours of Employment Regulations (HOER 2005)\nGoverned by Sections 130 to 134 of the Railways Act, 1989:\n1. **Intensive (Section 130(a)):** Work strenuous, continuous concentration (Section Controllers, Heavy Cabin Operators). Statutory limit: **42 hours/week** (average over 2 weeks). Periodic rest: **30 consecutive hours**.\n2. **Continuous (Section 130(b)):** Regular continuous duty (Station Masters, Loco Pilots, Clerks). Statutory limit: **54 hours/week**. Periodic rest: **30 consecutive hours**.\n3. **Essentially Intermittent (EI - Section 130(c)):** Work involves long periods of inaction (C-class Gatemen, Waiting room attendants). Statutory limit: **72 or 60 hours/week** (48 hrs work + 12/24 hrs split). Periodic rest: **24 consecutive hours**.\n4. **Excluded (Section 130(d)):** Managerial, supervisory, armed guards, railway school teachers.\n- **Overtime Calculation (Section 132):** Overtime worked beyond statutory limits is paid at **2 times the ordinary rate of wages** ($2 \\times \\text{Hourly Rate}$).\n\n### 5. 7th CPC Pay Rules & MACP Scheme\n- **Pay Matrix:** Replaced Pay Bands & Grade Pay with 18 Levels. Fitment factor: **2.57**.\n- **Annual Increment:** 3% uniform rate granted on **1st January or 1st July** based on completing 6 months qualifying service.\n- **Modified Assured Career Progression (MACP):**\n  - Granted on completion of **10, 20, and 30 years** of continuous regular service without promotion.\n  - Financial upgradation to the immediate next Pay Level in the hierarchy.\n  - Mandatory APAR benchmark: **'Very Good'** for the last 5 years.\n",
