    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. The 3-Tier Permanent Negotiating Machinery (PNM)\nSet up in 1952 by agreement between the Railway Board and railway trade unions to resolve industrial disputes peacefully:\n1. **Divisional Level:**\n   - Meetings between Divisional Officers (chaired by DRM / ADRM) and Divisional representatives of recognized unions.\n   - Frequency: Once in **two months**.\n2. **Zonal Level:**\n   - Meetings between Principal Heads of Departments (chaired by GM / PCPO) and the central executive of recognized unions.\n   - Frequency: Once in **three months** (quarterly).\n3. **Railway Board Level:**\n   - Meetings between Railway Board Members and the National Federations (AIRF & NFIR).\n   - Frequency: Twice a year.\n   - **Ad-Hoc Tribunal:** If a matter of importance cannot be resolved at the Railway Board level and the Government agrees, it is referred to an Ad-Hoc Railway Tribunal consisting of a neutral Chairman (retired High Court/Supreme Court judge) with equal representation from both sides.\n\n### 2. Participation of Railway Employees in Management (PREM)\nSet up in 1977 to foster collaborative decision-making, improving productivity, customer satisfaction, passenger amenities, and safety:\n- **4 Tiers:**\n  1. Apex Council at Railway Board level (CRB, Board Members + Federation Chiefs).\n  2. Zonal Level PREM Committee (GM, PHODs + Zonal Union Leaders).\n  3. Divisional Level PREM Committee (DRM, Branch Officers + Divisional Union Leaders).\n  4. Workshop Level PREM Committee (CWM, Dy. CPOs + Workshop Union Leaders).\n- *Distinction from PNM:* PREM strictly discusses organizational growth, revenue enhancement, scrap disposal, cost reduction, and safety measures\u2014NOT individual grievances or disciplinary matters.\n\n### 3. Joint Consultative Machinery (JCM)\nModeled on the UK Whitley Councils for all Central Government employees:\n- **National Council:** Chaired by Cabinet Secretary (covers all central ministries).\n- **Departmental Council:** Chaired by Chairman & CEO Railway Board (focuses on railway-specific conditions of service).\n\n### 4. Impact of the Industrial Relations Code, 2020 (Mandatory for Exam!)\nThe Government consolidated 29 labour laws into 4 comprehensive codes. The IR Code, 2020 repeals:\n1. Trade Unions Act, 1926.\n2. Industrial Employment (Standing Orders) Act, 1946.\n3. Industrial Disputes Act, 1947.\n- **Key Modern Provisions for Railways:**\n  - **Mandatory 14-Day Notice for Strikes & Lockouts:** Under Section 62, no person employed in ANY industrial establishment shall go on strike without giving 14 days' notice, or during pendency of conciliation proceedings. The erstwhile distinction between 'Public Utility Service' and other industries is removed.\n  - **Recognition of Negotiating Union:** If a single trade union has 51% or more membership, it is recognized as the sole negotiating union. Otherwise, a Negotiating Council is formed with unions having >=20% support.\n  - **Grievance Redressal Committee (GRC):** Every establishment employing 20 or more workers must have a GRC with equal worker and employer representation.\n",
