    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Constitutional Architecture of Parliamentary Financial Control\nUnder the Constitution of India, ultimate financial control is vested in Parliament:\n- **Article 265:** No tax shall be levied or collected except by authority of law.\n- **Article 266:** Consolidated Fund of India and Public Account. All revenues received and loans raised flow into the Consolidated Fund.\n- **Article 266(3):** No moneys out of the Consolidated Fund of India shall be appropriated except in accordance with law and for the purposes and in the manner provided in the Constitution.\n- **Article 112:** The President shall in respect of every financial year cause to be laid before both Houses of Parliament the Annual Financial Statement (Budget).\n- **Article 114:** Appropriation Bills granting legal authority to withdraw funds from the Consolidated Fund of India.\n- **Article 151:** Audit reports of the Comptroller and Auditor General (C&AG) on Railways are laid before each House of Parliament.\n\n### 2. Canons of Financial Propriety (Para 116, IRFC Vol. I)\nEvery railway officer exercising financial powers must observe four cardinal principles:\n1. **Prudence & Vigilance (The Prudent Man Rule - Para 116(i)):**\n   > *The expenditure should not be prima facie more than the occasion demands. Every railway servant must exercise the same vigilance and care in respect of expenditure incurred from public moneys as a person of ordinary prudence would exercise in respect of expenditure of his own money.*\n2. **Sanction & Authority (Para 116(ii)):**\n   > *The expenditure should not be sanctioned by an authority which will accrue directly or indirectly to its own advantage.*\n3. **No Personal Benefit / Favors (Para 116(iii)):**\n   > *Public money should not be utilized for the benefit of a particular person or section of the community unless:*\n   - *The amount of expenditure involved is insignificant; or*\n   - *A claim for the amount could be enforced in a Court of Law; or*\n   - *The expenditure is in pursuance of a recognized policy or custom.*\n4. **Appropriation Bounds (Para 116(iv)):**\n   > *The amount of allowances granted to meet expenditure of a particular type should be so regulated that the allowances are not on the whole a source of profit to the recipients (e.g., Travelling Allowance, Daily Allowance).*\n\n### 3. Public Accountability & Parliamentary Committees\n- **Public Accounts Committee (PAC):**\n  - Consists of **22 members** (15 elected from Lok Sabha and 7 from Rajya Sabha).\n  - Chaired traditionally by a senior leader from the Opposition in Lok Sabha.\n  - Examines Appropriation Accounts and the Reports of the C&AG on Railways.\n  - Scrutinizes financial irregularities, cost overruns, wasteful expenditure, and infructuous contracts.\n- **Estimates Committee (30 Lok Sabha Members):** Examines whether economies, organizational improvements, or administrative reforms consistent with policy can be effected.\n- **Committee on Public Undertakings (COPU):** Examines railway PSUs (CRIS, IRCTC, IRCON, RITES, RVNL, DFCCIL, CONCOR).\n",
