    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. The Railway Budget Lifecycle & Post-Merger Framework\nFrom FY 2017-18, the 92-year-old practice of a separate Railway Budget was discontinued based on the recommendations of the **Bibek Debroy Committee**. The Ministry of Railways' budget is now an integral part of the Union Budget under **Demand for Grants No. 85**.\n\n```\n[August Review]  ---> [Revised Estimates (RE) & BE] ---> [Parliamentary Enactment] ---> [Final Modification (FM)]\n(July/August)          (November/December)                (February - March)             (February/March)\nInitial course         Realistic revision of              Appropriation Act              Surrender of savings\ncorrection             current year targets               passed                         and final adjustments\n```\n\n### 2. Stages of Budgetary Reviews in Railways (IRFC Vol. I Paras 352 to 375)\n1. **August Review (July-August - Para 352):**\n   - Conducted after first quarter working results are compiled (April to June/July).\n   - Examines if earnings targets are being met and if expenditure is within proportional budget allotment.\n   - Highlights need for mid-course austerity or revenue generation drives.\n2. **Revised Estimates (RE) of Current Year & Budget Estimates (BE) of Next Year (Nov-Dec - Paras 356-368):**\n   - Submitted to Railway Board and Ministry of Finance.\n   - RE refines the budget based on actual expenditure for the first 6-7 months.\n   - BE forecasts requirements for the upcoming financial year.\n3. **Final Modification (FM) / Supplementary Grants (Feb-March - Paras 371-375):**\n   - Submitted in late February / early March based on 10 months' actuals.\n   - Identifies inescapable excess expenditure requiring supplementary grants from Parliament, or surrenders savings which cannot be utilized before March 31st.\n   - Any surrender after FM is considered bad budgeting and lapses at midnight on March 31.\n\n### 3. Key Budgetary Terminology\n- **Voted Expenditure:** Expenditure subject to the vote of Parliament (bulk of operational and capital expenses).\n- **Charged Expenditure:** Non-votable expenditure charged directly to the Consolidated Fund of India (satisfaction of court decrees, arbitration awards, CAT judgements).\n- **Demand for Grants:** Formal request made to Lok Sabha for sanction of funds under specific heads.\n- **Appropriation Act:** Statute passed by Parliament authorizing withdrawal of funds from the Consolidated Fund.\n",
    "deprecated_vs_modern": {
      "deprecated_practice": "Erstwhile 16 separate Demands for Grants (Demand 1 to 16) specific to Railways; separate Railway Budget speech by Railway Minister.",
      "modern_practice": "Single Demand for Grants (Demand No. 85 for Ministry of Railways) presented as part of Union Budget; internal allocation tracked via Plan Heads and Revenue Abstracts.",
      "transition_notes": "Abolition of dividend payment to General Revenues since 2017-18 (Railways relieved of dividend liability)."
    },
