    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Rules of Allocation (IRFC Vol. I Chapter IV, Paras 401 to 448)\nCapitalization principles dictate which fund bears an expenditure:\n1. **Capital (Cap - Para 402):** Expenditure for creating new assets or enhancing the capacity of existing assets (New Lines, Doubling, Initial Rolling Stock). Funded from General Capital Support / Gross Budgetary Support (GBS) or Market Borrowings (EBR).\n2. **Depreciation Reserve Fund (DRF - Para 408):** Expenditure on replacement and renewal of existing assets without improvement.\n3. **Development Fund (DF - Para 412):** Expenditure on passenger amenities, employee welfare works costing above specified limits, and unremunerative operational improvement works. (Funded from railway excess/surplus).\n4. **Rashtriya Rail Sanraksha Kosh (RRSK):** Dedicated non-lapsable critical safety fund created in 2017-18 with Rs. 1 lakh crore corpus over 5 years (renewed) for track renewal, signaling upgrades (Kavach), bridge rehabilitation.\n5. **Open Line Works Revenue (OLWR - Para 410):** Works costing below minor work ceilings charged directly to ordinary working revenue expenses.\n\n### 2. Classification of Revenue Expenditure (Abstracts A to L)\nRevenue expenditure is categorized under alphabetical Abstracts representing departments:\n- **Abstract A:** General Superintendence and Services (Administration / HQ / DRM offices).\n- **Abstract B:** Repairs and Maintenance of Permanent Way and Works (Civil Engg).\n- **Abstract C:** Repairs and Maintenance of Motive Power (Loco Sheds - Diesel & Electric).\n- **Abstract D:** Repairs and Maintenance of Carriages and Wagons (C&W Depots & Workshops).\n- **Abstract E:** Repairs and Maintenance of Plant and Equipment (Signaling, Telecom, Electrical General).\n- **Abstract F:** Operating Expenses - Rolling Stock and Equipment.\n- **Abstract G:** Operating Expenses - Traffic (Station staff, Guards, Controllers).\n- **Abstract H:** Operating Expenses - Fuel (Diesel, Electric traction energy).\n- **Abstract J:** Staff Welfare and Amenities (Medical, Health, SBF, Institutes).\n- **Abstract K:** Miscellaneous Working Expenses (Catering, Compensation claims, Security/RPF).\n- **Abstract L:** Suspense and Miscellaneous Receipts.\n- **Primary Units (PU):** Represent nature of expense: PU 01 (Salaries), PU 02 (Dearness Allowance), PU 03 (House Rent Allowance), PU 27 (Cost of Materials from Stock), PU 28 (Direct Purchases), PU 32 (Contractual Payments).\n\n### 3. Operating Ratio (OR)\nThe premier index of financial health and operational efficiency of Indian Railways:\n$$\\text{Operating Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total Working Expenses (TWE)}}{\\text{Gross Traffic Receipts (GTR)}} \\times 100$$\n- Where:\n  $$\\text{Total Working Expenses} = \\text{Ordinary Working Expenses (OWE)} + \\text{Appropriation to DRF} + \\text{Appropriation to Pension Fund}$$\n  $$\\text{Gross Traffic Receipts} = \\text{Gross Earnings (Passenger + Freight + Other Coaching + Sundry)} + \\text{Variation in Suspense (Traffic Book)}$$\n- **Interpretation:** An Operating Ratio of **98%** indicates that Railways spends 98 paise to earn 100 paise (leaving a surplus margin of only 2 paise). A lower Operating Ratio signifies greater financial efficiency and internal resource generation.\n\n### 4. Exchequer Control (IR Accounts Code Vol. I Paras 801 to 830)\n- Mechanism to monitor and control actual cash disbursement on a day-to-day and month-to-month basis against cash limits authorized by the Railway Board.\n- Prevents cash overdrafts and maintains financial discipline.\n- Implemented digitally through the Cash Authorization module in IPAS.\n",
