    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Selective Inventory Control Techniques (Stores Code)\nIndian Railways manages over 100,000 distinct items (spares, fuels, lubricants, rails, sleeper fastenings, electronics):\n\n```\n                        INVENTORY MATRICES\n   \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n   \u2502 ABC Analysis (Value):     A (70%)  |  B (20%)  | C (10%)\n   \u2502 VED Analysis (Critical):  Vital    | Essential | Desirable\n   \u2502 FSN Analysis (Velocity):  Fast     | Slow      | Non-Moving\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n1. **ABC Analysis (Based on Annual Usage Value):**\n   - **Category 'A':** Top ~10% of items accounting for ~70% of total annual consumption value. Managed with tightest control, low buffer stock, frequent review by Senior Officers (PCMM / CMM).\n   - **Category 'B':** Next ~20% of items accounting for ~20% of annual value. Moderate review.\n   - **Category 'C':** Remaining ~70% of items accounting for only ~10% of annual value. Bulk purchase, annual buffer, simplified ordering to save administrative overhead.\n2. **VED Analysis (Based on Operational Criticality):**\n   - **Vital (V):** Items whose absence causes immediate breakdown or affects safety (brake blocks, signal relays, wheelsets). Must have 100% availability; zero tolerance for stock-out.\n   - **Essential (E):** Spares causing moderate performance deterioration or delays within a few hours/days.\n   - **Desirable (D):** Non-critical items whose absence causes minor inconvenience (coach curtains, decorative trim).\n3. **Combined Matrix (e.g., AV, BV, CV):**\n   - **AV Items:** Vital item + High Value -> Maximum executive attention, long-term rate contracts, automated safety triggers.\n\n### 2. Turn Over Ratio (TOR)\nThe standard metric to gauge inventory management efficiency in Stores Depots:\n$$\\text{Turn Over Ratio (TOR)} = \\frac{\\text{Closing Balance of Stores as on March 31st}}{\\text{Total Value of Stores Issued during the Financial Year}} \\times 100$$\n- A **lower percentage** indicates better inventory turnover and reduced capital blockage.\n- Indian Railways strives to maintain TOR under **12% to 15%** (excluding fuel/rolling stock).\n\n### 3. Stock Verification & Discrepancies\n- **Stock Verifiers (SVs):** Specialized cadre under the Principal Financial Adviser (PFA / Accounts Department).\n- Independent physical verification of depot and departmental stock.\n- Discrepancy reporting:\n  - **Stock Sheets:** Issued when shortages or excesses are detected.\n  - Executive department must explain discrepancies within 30 days.\n  - Shortages not satisfactorily explained are recovered from responsible custodians.\n",
