    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Workshop Manufacturing Suspense (WMS) Account (IRFC I Chapter XV)\nA dedicated suspense head under capital expenditure (Plan Head 42 / 7200) through which all financial transactions of a production or repair workshop are routed:\n- **Debit to WMS:**\n  1. Direct labor wages (from attendance/job cards).\n  2. Direct materials drawn from stores depots.\n  3. Proportionate oncost charges (indirect costs).\n  4. Contractual work expenses.\n- **Credit to WMS:**\n  - Credited when the finished product or POH (Periodic Overhaul) coach/wagon/locomotive is inspected, passed, and dispatched to the user division/railway.\n  - The corresponding amount is debited to the Revenue allocation of the receiving division (Abstract D for wagons/coaches, Abstract C for locos).\n- **Year-End Balances:** Any uncleared balance in WMS represents Work-in-Progress (WIP) and must be reviewed to avoid unauthorized accumulation.\n\n### 2. Oncost Charges (Indirect Costs / Overheads)\nWorkshops incur substantial indirect expenses that cannot be charged directly to a single job. These are recovered through oncost percentages:\n1. **Shop Oncost:**\n   - Expenses confined to a specific shop (tool room power, shop cleaning, lubricants, supervisor pay, shop crane maintenance).\n   - Expressed as a percentage of direct labor cost of that shop:\n     $$\\text{Shop Oncost \\%} = \\frac{\\text{Total Shop Indirect Expenses}}{\\text{Total Direct Labor Cost of Shop}} \\times 100$$\n2. **General Oncost:**\n   - Expenses common to the entire workshop (CWM office administration, workshop electrical substation, railway hospital charges, security/RPF, yard shunting).\n   - Expressed as a percentage of total direct labor wages of all shops.\n3. **Proforma Oncost:**\n   - Elements not directly borne by the workshop budget but representing true economic costs (depreciation on workshop buildings and machinery, interest on capital, pension liability, audit charges).\n   - Charged when work is performed for outside parties, non-railway departments, or private commercial wagon builders.\n\n### 3. Financial Vetting of Incentive Rates\n- The Accounts Officer (WAO - Workshop Accounts Officer) vets the Allowed Time (AT) fixed by the Production Control Organisation (PCO).\n- Ensures that incentive bonus payments do not exceed the statutory ceiling of **50% of basic wages**.\n- Monitors that increase in productivity actually results in reduced overtime (OT) and lower unit cost of POH.\n",
