    "key_concepts": [
      "Classification of Losses: Losses of cash, stores, revenue, and damage to railway assets",
      "Mandatory Reporting (Para 202): Immediate report to Board and PDA for losses exceeding Rs. 50,000",
      "Investigation: Joint inquiry, departmental fact-finding, or reference to Vigilance / CBI",
      "Writing-off Losses: Sanction of competent authority required; cannot write off where negligence is established without disciplinary action",
      "Recovery: Loss recovered from delinquent employees under RS (D&A) Rules 1968"
    ],
    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Classification of Financial Losses (IRFC Vol. I Chapter II)\nUnder Chapter II of IRFC Vol. I, losses are classified into:\n1. **Losses of Cash:** Shortage in cash remitted by booking clerks, station masters, cashier theft, duplicate payment frauds.\n2. **Losses of Stores:** Pilferage from stores depots, theft of brass fittings, copper overhead wire theft, missing track fittings.\n3. **Losses of Revenue:** Under-invoicing of freight, uncollected demurrage/wharfage, fare evasion, unrecovered siding charges.\n4. **Damage to Railway Property:** Collisions, derailments, floods, fires in coaches or depots.\n\n### 2. Procedures for Reporting & Action (Para 202)\n- **Immediate Report:** Whenever any loss or embezzlement is discovered, the departmental officer must send an immediate preliminary report to:\n  - The Principal Financial Adviser (PFA).\n  - The General Manager.\n  - The Principal Director of Audit (PDA / Statutory Audit).\n  - The Railway Board (if monetary loss exceeds **Rs. 50,000** or involves new method of fraud).\n- **Inquiry:**\n  - Administrative enquiry to ascertain the exact quantum of loss, the modus operandi, and the specific officials responsible.\n  - If a criminal offense (forgery, bribery, theft) is prima facie established, the case must be referred to the Police / CBI.\n\n### 3. Rules for Regularization & Writing-off\n- **Basic Principle:** A loss should never be written off routinely. Every effort must be made to recover the loss from the person responsible or his sureties.\n- **Competent Authority:**\n  - Powers to write off irrecoverable losses are delegated in the Model SOP based on whether the loss is due to:\n    - *Theft, fraud, or negligence:* Lower write-off limits; strictly requires completion of DAR action.\n    - *Other causes (force majeure, accident, fair wear and tear):* Higher write-off limits.\n- Writing off of losses does NOT relieve the administration of the duty to take disciplinary action against negligent staff.\n",
