    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Master Summary of Asymptotic Time & Space Complexity\n| Data Structure / Algorithm | Access | Search | Insertion | Deletion | Space Complexity | Stable? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Array** | $O(1)$ | $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ | - |\n| **Singly Linked List** | $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ | $O(1)$ [head] | $O(1)$ [head] | $O(n)$ | - |\n| **Stack / Queue** | - | $O(n)$ | $O(1)$ | $O(1)$ | $O(n)$ | - |\n| **Binary Search Tree (BST)** | - | Avg $O(\\log n)$, Worst $O(n)$ | Avg $O(\\log n)$, Worst $O(n)$ | Avg $O(\\log n)$, Worst $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ | - |\n| **AVL Tree / Red-Black Tree** | - | $O(\\log n)$ | $O(\\log n)$ | $O(\\log n)$ | $O(n)$ | - |\n| **B-Tree / B+ Tree** | - | $O(\\log_B n)$ | $O(\\log_B n)$ | $O(\\log_B n)$ | $O(n)$ | - |\n| **Hash Table** | - | Avg $O(1)$, Worst $O(n)$ | Avg $O(1)$, Worst $O(n)$ | Avg $O(1)$, Worst $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ | - |\n| **Bubble Sort** | - | - | - | - | $O(1)$ aux | Yes |\n| **Selection Sort** | - | - | - | - | $O(1)$ aux | No |\n| **Insertion Sort** | - | - | - | - | $O(1)$ aux | Yes |\n| **Merge Sort** | - | - | - | - | $O(n)$ aux | Yes |\n| **QuickSort** | - | - | - | - | $O(\\log n)$ stack | No |\n| **Heap Sort** | - | - | - | - | $O(1)$ aux | No |\n\n### 2. Core Graph Algorithms in Railway Route Planning\n```\n       BFS (Breadth-First Search)                  DIJKSTRA'S ALGORITHM\n  - Uses Queue (FIFO)                        - Uses Min-Priority Queue (Heap)\n  - Explores layer-by-layer                  - Calculates shortest paths in weighted graph\n  - Finds shortest path in unweighted graph  - Constraint: All edge weights must be non-negative\n  - Time: O(V + E)                           - Time: O((V + E) log V) with binary heap\n```\n- **Dijkstra's Shortest Path:**\n  - Maintains a distance array `dist[]` initialized to $\\infty$, with `dist[src] = 0`.\n  - In each step, extracts the vertex $u$ with the minimum tentative distance from a min-heap.\n  - Relaxes all outgoing edges $(u, v)$ with weight $w$:\n    $$\\text{if } dist[u] + w < dist[v] \\implies dist[v] = dist[u] + w$$\n  - Directly utilized in Indian Railways train pathing, FOIS rake dispatch route rationalization, and telecom optical fiber routing.\n\n### 3. B+ Tree Architecture in Database Storage\n```\n                           [ 50 | 100 ]            <- Internal Node (Routing keys only)\n                          /     |                               /      |                      [ 20 | 35 ]  [ 65 | 80 ]  [ 120 | 150 ]\n               /    |   \\   /    |   \\   /     |                   \u25bc     \u25bc    \u25bc \u25bc     \u25bc    \u25bc \u25bc      \u25bc      \u25bc\n Leaf Nodes: [10,20]->[25,35]->[50,65]->[70,80]->[100,120]->[130,150]\n              |       |        |        |         |          |\n              \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u25ba Doubly Linked List of Records\n```\n- **Why B+ Trees over BSTs or AVL Trees for Database Indexes?**\n  1. **Massive Fan-out ($B \\ge 100$):** Keeps the tree height extremely small ($3$ to $4$ levels for billions of rows), minimizing high-latency disk seeks.\n  2. **Data Pointers Exclusively in Leaves:** Internal nodes contain only routing keys, allowing thousands of keys to fit into a single 8 KB memory page.\n  3. **Leaf Sequential Linked List:** Range scans (`WHERE train_date BETWEEN '2026-01-01' AND '2026-01-31'`) require only locating the first leaf via binary search, followed by high-speed sequential pointer traversal along the leaf chain.\n",
    "deprecated_vs_modern": {
      "deprecated_practice": "Manual tape sorting (multi-pass external merge sorts on reel tapes); linear file scanning of sequential flat files; unindexed flat master files.",
      "modern_practice": "B+ Tree and Hash indexing in clustered DBMS; in-memory data structures (Skip Lists in Redis, Radix Trees in Linux kernel); distributed graph databases (Neo4j) for network routing.",
      "transition_notes": "All passenger and freight tracking systems in CRIS use B+ Tree-indexed relational tables running on high-speed SAN/NVMe storage."
    }
