    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Process Scheduling & State Transitions\n```\n                 Admit               Dispatch (Scheduler)\n       [ New ] \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba [ Ready ] \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba [ Running ] \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba [ Terminated ]\n                            \u25b2                                 \u2502  Exit\n                            \u2502         I/O or Event Wait       \u2502\n                            \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 [ Waiting / Blocked ]\n                                     I/O Complete\n```\n- **Process Control Block (PCB):** Data structure maintaining PID, Process State, Program Counter, CPU registers, Memory management limits, Open file descriptors, and CPU scheduling priority.\n- **Deadlock Coffman Conditions:** A deadlock occurs if and only if all four conditions hold simultaneously:\n  1. *Mutual Exclusion:* At least one resource held in a non-shareable mode.\n  2. *Hold and Wait:* A process holds at least one resource while waiting to acquire additional resources.\n  3. *No Preemption:* Resources cannot be forcibly seized; only released voluntarily.\n  4. *Circular Wait:* A closed chain of processes exists where $P_0$ waits for $P_1$, $P_1$ waits for $P_2$, ..., and $P_n$ waits for $P_0$.\n\n### 2. Virtual Memory & Page Translation Architecture\n```\n Logical Address (from CPU):\n  [ Virtual Page Number (VPN) ] [ Offset ]\n                 \u2502                  \u2502\n                 \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510          \u2502\n                 \u25bc       \u25bc          \u2502\n              [ TLB ]  (Hit?) \u2500\u2500\u25ba Frame Number\n                 \u2502 (Miss)           \u2502\n                 \u25bc                  \u2502\n         [ Page Table in RAM ]      \u2502\n                 \u2502                  \u2502\n                 \u25bc                  \u25bc\n     Physical Address: [ Frame Number ] [ Offset ] \u2500\u2500\u25ba System RAM\n```\n- **Page Fault Handling:** If the valid/invalid bit in the Page Table is 0 (page not in RAM), a hardware trap to the OS occurs. The OS locates the page on the swap partition/pagefile, selects a victim frame using LRU replacement, writes the victim to disk if dirty, reads the requested page into the frame, updates the Page Table bit to 1, and restarts the instruction.\n- **Thrashing:** Occurs when the sum of process working sets exceeds total physical RAM. The CPU spends virtually 100% of its cycles servicing page faults rather than executing user instructions. Resolved by suspending processes.\n\n### 3. Unix File System (Inode) vs Windows NTFS\n| Feature | Unix / Linux (EXT4 / XFS) | Windows (NTFS) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Core Metadata Structure** | **Inode:** Contains file mode, UID, GID, size, timestamps, and 12 direct block pointers, 1 single indirect, 1 double indirect, 1 triple indirect pointer. | **Master File Table (MFT):** Every file is a record in the MFT. Small files (<1 KB) stored directly inside the MFT record (*resident files*). |\n| **Max File Size** | EXT4: 16 TB; XFS: 8 Exabytes | NTFS: 16 Terabytes (theoretical 8 PB) |\n| **Integrity & Journaling** | Journaling (Data, Ordered, or Writeback mode) | Transactional USN (Update Sequence Number) Journal with self-healing |\n| **Access Security** | POSIX permissions (Read/Write/Execute for Owner/Group/Others) + POSIX ACLs + SELinux / AppArmor contexts | Windows Security Descriptors, Security Identifier (SID), Discretionary Access Control Lists (DACLs) |\n\n### 4. Unix Command-Line Mastery: vi, grep, awk & Shell Scripting\n#### Useful Commands & Syntaxes:\n- **vi Modes:** `Esc` enters Command mode; `i` or `a` enters Insert mode; `:` enters Ex/Last-line mode. Save and exit: `:wq` or `ZZ`. Search: `/pattern`. Replace all: `:%s/old/new/g`.\n- **Octal Permissions:** `chmod 755 script.sh` (Owner: rwx = 4+2+1=7; Group: r-x = 4+1=5; Others: r-x = 4+1=5).\n- **`grep`:** Global Regular Expression Print: `grep -E 'ERROR|CRITICAL' /var/log/syslog`.\n- **`awk`:** Pattern scanning and processing language:\n  ```bash\n  # Print username and shell of users with UID >= 1000 from /etc/passwd\n  awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 { print \"User: \" $1 \" | Shell: \" $7 }' /etc/passwd\n  ```\n- **Production Shell Script Template for Automated Railway Log Backup:**\n  ```bash\n  #!/bin/bash\n  # Automated Railway EDP Daily Log Archival Script\n  BACKUP_DIR=\"/backup/edp_logs/$(date +%Y%m%d)\"\n  LOG_SRC=\"/var/log/railway_apps\"\n  mkdir -p \"$BACKUP_DIR\"\n  tar -czf \"$BACKUP_DIR/logs_$(date +%H%M%S).tar.gz\" -C \"$LOG_SRC\" .\n  # Retain only last 30 days backups\n  find /backup/edp_logs/ -type d -mtime +30 -exec rm -rf {} +\n  echo \"Backup completed successfully at $(date)\" >> /var/log/edp_backup.log\n  ```\n",
