    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Database Normalization Step-by-Step\n```\n  [ Unnormalized Table ]\n            \u2502 (Eliminate repeating groups, ensure atomic scalar values)\n            \u25bc\n  [ 1st Normal Form (1NF) ]\n            \u2502 (Eliminate partial dependencies: All non-key attributes fully functionally dependent on entire PK)\n            \u25bc\n  [ 2nd Normal Form (2NF) ]\n            \u2502 (Eliminate transitive dependencies: Non-key attributes must not depend on other non-key attributes)\n            \u25bc\n  [ 3rd Normal Form (3NF) ]\n            \u2502 (For every dependency X -> Y, X must be a Super Key)\n            \u25bc\n  [ Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF) ]\n```\n\n### 2. Transaction Isolation Levels & Concurrency Anomalies\n| Isolation Level | Dirty Read | Non-Repeatable Read | Phantom Read | Implementation Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Read Uncommitted** | Possible | Possible | Possible | No read locks; reads uncommitted dirty data. |\n| **Read Committed** | **Prevented** | Possible | Possible | Short-term read locks released immediately after read, or MVCC consistent snapshot. (Default in Oracle / PostgreSQL) |\n| **Repeatable Read** | **Prevented** | **Prevented** | Possible | Long-term read and write locks held until transaction ends. (Default in MySQL InnoDB) |\n| **Serializable** | **Prevented** | **Prevented** | **Prevented** | Range locks / Strict 2PL / Snapshot Isolation with serializable conflict detection. |\n\n### 3. Production SQL Mastery with Examples\n#### A. High-Value Railway Analytics Query (Window Functions & Joins)\n```sql\n-- Rank top 3 passenger revenue generating trains per railway zone for FY 2025-26\nWITH ZoneTrainEarnings AS (\n    SELECT \n        z.zone_code,\n        t.train_no,\n        t.train_name,\n        SUM(b.fare_amount) AS total_revenue,\n        DENSE_RANK() OVER (\n            PARTITION BY z.zone_code \n            ORDER BY SUM(b.fare_amount) DESC\n        ) AS revenue_rank\n    FROM railway_zones z\n    JOIN railway_divisions d ON z.zone_id = d.zone_id\n    JOIN train_masters t ON d.div_id = t.primary_div_id\n    JOIN ticket_bookings b ON t.train_no = b.train_no\n    WHERE b.booking_date BETWEEN '2025-04-01' AND '2026-03-31'\n      AND b.ticket_status = 'CONFIRMED'\n    GROUP BY z.zone_code, t.train_no, t.train_name\n)\nSELECT zone_code, revenue_rank, train_no, train_name, total_revenue\nFROM ZoneTrainEarnings\nWHERE revenue_rank <= 3\nORDER BY zone_code, revenue_rank;\n```\n\n#### B. Indexing Trade-Offs: B+ Tree vs Bitmap Index\n- **B+ Tree Index:** Ideal for high-cardinality columns (e.g., `pnr_no`, `aadhaar_hash`, `employee_id`). Supports high-speed equality searches and range scans.\n- **Bitmap Index:** Ideal for low-cardinality columns with millions of rows (e.g., `gender` [M/F/T], `booking_quota` [GN/TQ/LD], `coach_class` [1A/2A/3A/SL]). Uses compact bit vectors; logical boolean operations (`AND`, `OR`) performed at hardware bit level. Highly efficient for DSS/Data Warehouses, but poor for heavy OLTP concurrency due to row-locking serialization.\n",
