    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Chronological Evolution & Statutory Architecture of Indian Cyber Law\n```\n 1996: UNCITRAL Model Law on Electronic Commerce (UNGA Res. 51/162)\n   \u2502\n   \u25bc\n 2000: Information Technology Act, 2000 (Act No. 21 of 2000)\n   \u2502   - Legal recognition for electronic transactions, digital signatures\n   \u25bc\n 2008: Information Technology (Amendment) Act, 2008 (Effective Oct 2009)\n   \u2502   - Added Sec 66F (Cyber Terrorism), Sec 70A (NCIIPC), Sec 70B (CERT-In)\n   \u25bc\n 2015: Landmark Judgment: Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (5 SCC 1)\n   \u2502   - Section 66A struck down as unconstitutional (Article 19(1)(a) breach)\n   \u25bc\n 2017: Nine-Judge Bench: Justice K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (10 SCC 1)\n   \u2502   - Right to Privacy affirmed as a Fundamental Right under Article 21\n   \u25bc\n 2022: CERT-In Mandatory Directions under Section 70B(6)\n   \u2502   - 6-Hour incident reporting; 180-day log retention within Indian borders\n   \u25bc\n 2023: Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (Act No. 22 of 2023)\n       - Comprehensive personal data privacy framework; penalties up to \u20b9250 Crore\n```\n\n### 2. Deep Dive: Key Sections of the Information Technology Act, 2000\n| Section | Legal Heading / Offence | Nature of Offence & Statutory Sanction | Practical Railway Application |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Section 43** | Penalty and compensation for damage to computer systems | Civil liability; compensation up to \u20b91 Crore to affected person for unauthorized copying, extraction, or virus introduction. | Staff extracting unauthorized UTS/PRS passenger booking databases. |\n| **Section 66** | Computer related offences (dishonestly or fraudulently committing acts in Sec 43) | Imprisonment up to **3 years**, or fine up to **\u20b95 Lakhs**, or both. Cognizable and bailable. | Hacking into railway booking portals or tampering with IPAS salary records. |\n| **Section 66A** | Punishment for sending offensive messages through communication service | **STRUCK DOWN AS UNCONSTITUTIONAL** by Supreme Court in *Shreya Singhal (2015)*. Cannot be invoked. | Frequently tested in competitive exams regarding its unconstitutional status. |\n| **Section 66C** | Punishment for identity theft | Imprisonment up to **3 years** and fine up to **\u20b91 Lakh**. | Fraudulent use of another officer's digital signature token (DSC) or credentials. |\n| **Section 66D** | Cheating by personation by using computer resource | Imprisonment up to **3 years** and fine up to **\u20b91 Lakh**. | Fake railway recruitment portals deceiving job applicants. |\n| **Section 66F** | Punishment for **Cyber Terrorism** | **IMPRISONMENT FOR LIFE**. | Denial-of-service attack on railway signaling, PRS, or power grid SCADA. |\n| **Section 70** | Protected Systems / Critical Information Infrastructure | Imprisonment up to **10 years** and fine. Cognizable and non-bailable. | Unauthorized intrusion into notified railway train control and PRS databases. |\n| **Section 70A** | National Critical Information Infrastructure Protection Centre | Statutory establishment of **NCIIPC** under NTRO as nodal agency for CII. | Coordinates railway cybersecurity drills with CRIS and Railway Board. |\n| **Section 70B** | Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT-In) | Statutory national agency for cybersecurity incident response and directions. | Issues security advisories, vulnerability alerts, and incident directives. |\n\n### 3. CERT-In Directions (28th April 2022) Mandates\n1. **6-Hour Mandatory Incident Reporting:** Any organization (including Railways) must report cybersecurity incidents (e.g., ransomware attacks, data breaches, identity theft, unauthorized access) to CERT-In within **6 hours** of noticing.\n2. **NTP Clock Synchronization:** All ICT devices, servers, routers, and firewalls must synchronize system clocks strictly with Indian Standard Time (IST) via National Physical Laboratory (NPL) or NIC NTP servers.\n3. **180-Day ICT Log Preservation:** All system logs, firewall logs, user access logs, and application audit trails must be preserved for **180 days** and maintained within the territorial jurisdiction of India.\n4. **5-Year Customer KYC Records:** Cloud, VPS, and VPN providers must retain validated user registration details for a minimum of **5 years**.\n\n### 4. Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDP Act)\n- **Act No. 22 of 2023**, passed by Parliament in August 2023.\n- **Key Definitions:**\n  - *Data Principal:* The individual to whom the personal data relates.\n  - *Data Fiduciary:* Any person/entity that determines the purpose and means of processing personal data (e.g., Indian Railways / CRIS).\n  - *Significant Data Fiduciary (SDF):* Entities notified based on data volume, sensitivity, and risk to sovereignty; must appoint a Data Protection Officer (DPO) based in India and conduct independent audits.\n- **Statutory Penalties (Schedule of the Act):**\n  - Failure to take reasonable security safeguards to prevent personal data breach: Fine up to **\u20b9250 Crore**.\n  - Failure to notify the Data Protection Board and affected Data Principals of a personal data breach: Fine up to **\u20b9200 Crore**.\n  - Non-compliance with specific obligations regarding children's personal data: Fine up to **\u20b9200 Crore**.\n",
